import object_detector

def calculate_distance():
    # Import objectDetector tool
    Detector_Tool = object_detector.Object_Detector_Tool

    # Define the input image
    image_path = "/path/to/image.png"

    # Detect objects
    labels = ["baseball", "basket"]
    detector_tool = Detector_Tool(image_path, languages=['en', 'de'], detail=1)

    # Process the detection results
    result = detector_tool.execute()

    # Get the detected objects
    detected_objects = result[0]

    # Extract coordinates of the detected objects
    obj_bbx = list(map(lambda i: (i[0][0] + i[0][2], i[0][1] + i[0][3], i[1][0] + i[1][2], i[1][1] + i[1][3]), detected_objects))

    # Calculate distance between the sofa and the other objects
    sofa_bbx = [bounding_box[0] for bounding_box in detected_objects if bounding_box[1]["label"] == "baseball"]
    distance = len(sofa_bbx[0])  # Assuming there is only one baseball object per object

    print(f"Distance between sofa and baseball: {distance}")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    calculate_distance()
```

This Python program uses the `Object_Detector_Tool` from the `object_detector` module to detect objects in the image and calculates the distance between the sofa and the baseball object. The resulting distance is then printed. Please make sure to replace `"/path/to/image.png"` with the actual path of your image.

If you want to calculate the distance between the sofa and the other objects, replace the check `if ` in the if condition `bounding_box[0]` by `bounding_box[1]["label"] == "baseball"` and adjust the `distance` line accordingly.
